🚽 Test case: issue #389 x #375 -- writing a deadass array through its
🚽 decayed name.
🚽
🚽 `*cx = v` and `*(cx + i) = v` are the same write as `cx[i] = v`, which
🚽 #381 rejects during semantic analysis. Before decay they did not
🚽 compile at all ("Cannot dereference a non-pointer expression"); decay
🚽 must not turn them into a way around const. The check runs before the
🚽 operand decays -- afterwards the target is a dereferenced pointer, and
🚽 writes through a pointer are deliberately not const-traced.
🚽
🚽 The const array is found structurally -- through any depth of pointer
🚽 arithmetic, or an explicit `&cx[i]` -- not by matching a list of
🚽 shapes. An earlier version recognized only `cx`, `cx +- e` and `e + cx`,
🚽 so `*(cx + 1 + 0) = 9` got through and printed `1 9 8` (#389 review).
🚽 The longer forms below are that regression.
🚽
🚽 The yapping() before the writes is the point, as in
🚽 semantic_error_const_array_no_side_effect: the harness compares stdout
🚽 when it is non-empty, so this fails if interpretation ever starts.

gang Box {
    rizz vals[3];
};

skibidi main {
    deadass rizz cx[3] = {1, 2, 3};
    deadass gang Box b;
    yapping("side effect");
    *cx = 3;
    *(cx + 1) = 4;
    *(1 + cx) = 5;
    *(cx + 1 + 0) = 9;
    *(cx + 0 + 2) = 8;
    *(2 + (cx - 1)) = 7;
    *(&cx[1]) = 6;
    *(&cx[0] + 1) = 5;
    *(b.vals + 1) = 4;
    bussin 0;
}
